From 5c933c5107e172fc08a5444ef9d018287826b9e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: autoresearch <agent@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 05:00:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/15] x-asr: build the chunk graph once and own its context
 (fixes a latent use-after-free)

Two things were wrong in the same place. First, build_chunk_graph() returned a ggml_cgraph whose
ggml_context it had already freed - ggml allocates mem_buffer even with no_alloc=true, so the graph struct
lived in freed memory. It only worked because the next ggml_init happened to reuse that block before anyone
touched the graph again. Second, the whole ~4,000-node graph was rebuilt and re-allocated on every decode
step (143 builds for 145 steps on a 69 s clip), costing 0.196 s of construction plus 0.349 s of
reset+alloc.

Now the context owns the graph: build once, ggml_backend_sched_reset + alloc_graph once, then per step only
write the inputs and compute again - the same graph-reuse pattern llama.cpp uses across tokens of a
streaming model. The positional-encoding tables are built once for the same reason (they are pure functions
of the geometry). xasr_free frees the scheduler before the graph context, since the scheduler's allocations
point into it.

Numerically this is a no-op: the same nodes, in the same order, with the same inputs. The host gate confirms
it - transcript hash 192184ebcd54977e, all three WERs, attribution and DER-lite unchanged.

Also adds host-phase timers to XASR_PROF (fbank / build / alloc / pos-enc / inputs / compute / outputs),
which is how the split was measured: the ASR leg is ~93% ggml compute (19.2 s compute vs 1.3 s of host work
per 69 s clip), i.e. the opposite of the diar leg, where 40% was signal processing.
